{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ac0f154488ed217b", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAnyBand: Unified Multi-Bandwidth Speech Extension via Frequency-Aware\n2\tIn-Context Spectral Infilling\n3\tJunchuan Zhao1 , Minh Duc Vu2 , Bowen Zhang3 , Ye Wang1∗\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\tSchool of Computing, National University of Singapore\n7\tDepartment of Statistics & Data Science, National University of Singapore\n8\t3\n9\tCollege of Computing and Data Science, Nanyang Technological University\n10\tjunchuan@u.nus.edu, minhduc.vu@u.nus.edu, bowen009@e.ntu.edu.sg, dcswangy@nus.edu.sg\n11\t2\n12\t\n13\tIntroduction\n14\tSpeech bandwidth is often constrained by acquisition devices, communication channels, compression systems, or\n15\tlegacy recording conditions. Bandwidth extension (BWE)\n16\taims to restore missing high-frequency content from bandlimited speech, thereby improving clarity, naturalness, and\n17\tperceptual quality. The task is inherently underdetermined,\n18\tsince multiple acoustically plausible high-frequency continuations may correspond to the same observed low-frequency\n19\tsignal (Yang et al. 2026). Despite substantial advances in\n20\tneural speech restoration and generative audio modeling,\n21\tsupporting diverse and continuously varying input cutoffs\n22\twithin a single BWE system remains challenging.\n23\t1\n24\t∗\n25\t\n26\thttps://danny-nus.github.io/AnyBand-DemoPage/\n27\tCorresponding author.\n28\t\n29\tcontext\n30\t\n31\tfuture speech\n32\t\n33\t“From Low Band to High Band”\n34\tmissing\n35\thigh-band\n36\t\n37\tMel Frequency\n38\t\n39\tBandwidth extension (BWE) aims to recover missing highfrequency content from band-limited speech. Existing methods often formulate BWE as a fixed or predefined bandwidth\n40\tconversion problem, potentially requiring cutoff-specific\n41\tmodels or retraining when the input bandwidth changes.\n42\tThis assumption limits their applicability to practical scenarios where speech may arrive with diverse cutoff frequencies. We propose AnyBand, a unified BWE framework\n43\tthat recasts bandwidth extension as in-context spectral infilling. Motivated by prompt-based zero-shot speech generation,\n44\tAnyBand conditions high-frequency generation on the observed low-frequency spectrum, using the available band as\n45\ta frequency-domain prompt that conveys content, speaker,\n46\tprosodic, and spectral-envelope cues. This formulation enables a single model to perform cutoff-conditioned generation\n47\tover a continuous range of input bandwidths. AnyBand is\n48\ttrained with missing-band conditional flow matching and an\n49\tEasy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum over continuously sampled cutoff frequencies. To better exploit the spectral prompt,\n50\twe introduce a frequency-aware Diffusion Transformer that\n51\tmodels cross-frequency interactions and long-range temporal dependencies, followed by a physically motivated multiview adversarial refinement stage to enhance spectral realism,\n52\tenvelope coherence, and harmonic consistency. Experiments\n53\ton multiple datasets and bandwidth settings show that AnyBand consistently improves spectral reconstruction over existing baselines while achieving competitive perceptual quality\n54\tacross both standard and irregular input cutoffs. Audio samples are available1 .\n55\t\n56\tBandwidth Continuation\n57\t\n58\tSpeech Continuation\n59\t\n60\tMel Frequency\n61\t\n62\tarXiv:2608.00572v1 [cs.SD] 1 Aug 2026\n63\t\n64\tAbstract\n65\t\n66\tTime\n67\t\n68\t2 kHz\n69\t\n70\tobserved\n71\tlow-band\n72\t\n73\tTime\n74\t\n75\t4 kHz\n76\t\n77\t8 kHz\n78\t\n79\t16 kHz\n80\t\n81\t“Different cutoffs, same continuation formulation!”\n82\t\n83\tFigure 1: Bandwidth extension as frequency-domain continuation across different cutoff frequencies.\n84\t\n85\tMany neural BWE and audio super-resolution methods have improved high-frequency reconstruction under\n86\tfixed or predefined bandwidth settings using convolutional,\n87\tWaveNet-based, flow-based, diffusion-based, adversarial, or\n88\tcodec-based generative models (Kuleshov, Enam, and Ermon 2017; Gupta et al. 2019; Li et al. 2021; Su et al. 2021;\n89\tLee and Han 2021; Zhang et al. 2021; Lu et al. 2024a; Fang\n90\tet al. 2025; Zhang et al. 2026). Although these approaches\n91\tachieve strong performance within their target bandwidth\n92\tconfigurations, their behavior is often closely coupled to the\n93\tsource–target bandwidth configurations covered during training. Adapting to a different input cutoff may therefore may\n94\trequire retraining, fine-tuning, or additional model specialization, limiting their flexibility in practical applications.\n95\tRecent works have explored unified, flexible, or blind\n96\tBWE and audio super-resolution systems that support multiple sampling rates, bandwidths, degradation conditions, or\n97\tunknown low-pass filters (Andreev et al. 2023; Han and\n98\tLee 2022; Liu et al. 2022a,b, 2024; Salhab and Harmanani\n99\t2025; Sharma 2026; Moliner, Elvander, and Välimäki 2024;\n100\tKim et al. 2024). Although these methods reduce the need\n101\tfor cutoff-specific models, they typically formulate varying\n102\tbandwidths as separate restoration, upsampling, or condi-\n103\t\n104\t\ftioning scenarios. We instead cast multi-bandwidth BWE\n105\tas a unified spectral-infilling problem, where the observed\n106\tlow-frequency spectrum provides acoustic context and a frequency mask specifies the missing region. As shown in\n107\tFigure 1, different cutoffs correspond to different amounts\n108\tof observed context under the same frequency-continuation\n109\tformulation. The model then reconstructs the missing highfrequency content while preserving the harmonic structure,\n110\tvoicing patterns, spectral envelope, and temporal dynamics\n111\tof the observed band.\n112\tWe propose AnyBand, a unified framework that formulates\n113\tBWE over continuously varying cutoff frequencies as incontext spectral infilling. Inspired by acoustic-context conditioning in prompt-based speech generation (Wang et al.\n114\t2023; Le et al. 2023; Chen et al. 2025; Eskimez et al. 2024;\n115\tZhao, Vu, and Wang 2026; Liang et al. 2026; Zhao et al.\n116\t2026), AnyBand treats the observed low-frequency spectrum as a frequency-domain prompt and uses an explicit\n117\tfrequency mask to specify the missing band. We refer to\n118\tthis formulation as in-context spectral infilling because the\n119\tobserved spectrum provides acoustic context for generating\n120\tthe unobserved frequency region. A frequency-aware Diffusion Transformer models cross-frequency interactions and\n121\tlong-range temporal dependencies, while Easy-to-Balanced\n122\tcutoff training and endpoint-focused adversarial refinement\n123\timprove performance across cutoff conditions and enhance\n124\tthe realism of the generated high-frequency content.\n125\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n126\t• We formulate multi-bandwidth speech extension as an\n127\tin-context spectral-infilling task, in which the observed\n128\tlow-frequency spectrum provides acoustic context and\n129\tan explicit frequency mask specifies the missing region\n130\tacross continuously varying cutoff frequencies.\n131\t• We propose a frequency-aware conditional flow architecture that explicitly models cross-frequency and longrange temporal dependencies, together with an Easy-toBalanced curriculum for continuously sampled cutoff frequencies.\n132\t• We introduce endpoint-focused multi-view adversarial\n133\trefinement with complementary objectives for spectral\n134\trealism, cross-band envelope coherence, and harmonic\n135\tconsistency.\n136\t\n137\tRelated Work\n138\tSpeech Bandwidth Extension and Super-Resolution\n139\tEarly neural BWE and audio super-resolution methods\n140\tmainly learned direct waveform- or spectrum-level mappings\n141\tfrom band-limited to full-band speech. (Kuleshov, Enam, and\n142\tErmon 2017) introduced a convolutional waveform model,\n143\twhile TFNet (Lim et al. 2018) jointly modeled time- and\n144\tfrequency-domain representations. Later work further improved efficiency for real-time BWE (Li et al. 2021).\n145\tGenerative methods were subsequently introduced to improve high-frequency reconstruction and perceptual quality, including autoregressive and neural-vocoder-based models (Gupta et al. 2019; Su et al. 2021), normalizing\n146\tflows (Zhang et al. 2021), and diffusion models (Lee and Han\n147\t\n148\t2021). Other studies directly predict magnitude, phase, or\n149\tcomplex spectra (Hu et al. 2020; Mandel, Tal, and Adi 2023;\n150\tLu et al. 2024a), often with adversarial supervision (Andreev et al. 2023; Lu et al. 2024a). Recent work also explores\n151\tquantized or codec-derived latent spaces (Fang et al. 2025;\n152\tZhang et al. 2026) and Schrödinger bridges (Kong et al.\n153\t2025). However, many of these systems remain specialized\n154\tfor fixed or predefined source–target bandwidth settings.\n155\t\n156\tUnified and Flexible Bandwidth Extension\n157\tRecent studies have developed unified systems for multiple\n158\tsampling rates, bandwidths, or degradation conditions. NUWave 2 (Han and Lee 2022) conditions a diffusion model on\n159\tinput bandwidth, while MS-BWE (Lu et al. 2024b) uses cascaded stages for flexible sampling-rate conversion. NVSR\n160\t(Liu et al. 2022a), VoiceFixer (Liu et al. 2022b), and AudioSR (Liu et al. 2024) address broader super-resolution\n161\tor restoration settings. Other work improves flexibility or\n162\tefficiency through adversarial training, diffusion distillation, flow matching, or flexible vocoders (Salhab and Harmanani 2025; Im and Nam 2025; Choi et al. 2026; Sharma\n163\t2026; Yun, Kim, and Lee 2025). Blind BWE further handles unknown degradations; BABE (Moliner, Elvander, and\n164\tVälimäki 2024) jointly estimates a low-pass filter and reconstructs full-band audio, but requires inference-time optimization.\n165\tA related direction uses partial acoustic context to guide\n166\tspeech generation. Voicebox (Le et al. 2023) infills missing\n167\ttemporal segments, while E2 TTS (Eskimez et al. 2024) and\n168\tF5-TTS (Chen et al. 2025) use flow matching conditioned on\n169\tobserved or reference speech. Inspired by this principle, AnyBand treats the observed low-frequency spectrum as acoustic context and uses an explicit frequency mask to formulate\n170\tvarying cutoffs as a common spectral-infilling task.\n171\t\n172\tProposed Method\n173\tProblem Formulation: In-Context Spectral Infilling\n174\tRather than treating each input cutoff as a separate\n175\tsource–target conversion setting, AnyBand formulates multibandwidth BWE as in-context spectral infilling. The observed low-frequency spectrum serves as acoustic context,\n176\twhile the masked high-frequency region specifies the region\n177\tto be reconstructed. Let M ∈ RF ×T denote the target logmel spectrogram, where F and T are the numbers of mel bins\n178\tand temporal frames. Given cutoff frequency fc and its melbin index kc , we define the missing-band mask M ∈ {0, 1}F\n179\tas Mk = 1[k > kc ], where zeros and ones denote observed\n180\tand missing bins, respectively. The masked observation is:\n181\tf = (1 − M) ⊙ M,\n182\tM\n183\t\n184\t(1)\n185\t\n186\tf preserves the\n187\twith the mask broadcast over time. Thus, M\n188\tspectrum below fc and removes the region to be reconstructed.\n189\tThe retained spectrum provides cues about content,\n190\tspeaker identity, prosody, and spectral structure, while its\n191\tfrequency extent specifies the BWE condition. Conditioned\n192\tf and auxiliary information\n193\ton the clean spectral prompt M\n194\t\n195\t\fLinear Projection\n196\tPositional\n197\tEmbedding\n198\t\n199\t+\n200\t\n201\tLayer Norm\n202\tScale, Shift\n203\tT-MHSA\n204\t\n205\tLayer Norm\n206\t\n207\tScale\n208\t\n209\tF-MHSA\n210\t\n211\tMasked\n212\t\n213\t+\n214\t\n215\tLayer Norm\n216\t\n217\tMLP\n218\t\n219\tScale, Shift\n220\t\n221\tAttention Pooling\n222\t\n223\tF0, V/UV\n224\t\n225\tLinear Projection\n226\t\n227\t…\n228\t\n229\tLinear Projection\n230\t\n231\tMLP\n232\tPositional\n233\tEmbedding\n234\t\n235\tTraining Strategy\n236\tEasy-to-Balanced Cutoff Annealing\n237\t\n238\t+\n239\t\n240\tF-Transformer Block\n241\t\n242\t+\n243\t\n244\tLayer Norm\n245\t\n246\t+\n247\tFeature Extractor\n248\t\n249\tFrequency Decoder\n250\t\n251\tDiT Block\n252\t\n253\tFrequency Encoder\n254\t\n255\tLinear Projection\n256\t\n257\tMLP\n258\tScale\n259\t\n260\t+\n261\tLayer Norm\n262\t\n263\tEmbedding\n264\t\n265\t+\n266\t\n267\tScale, Shift\n268\t\n269\tgradient\n270\tDiscriminators\n271\t\n272\tFigure 2: Overview of the proposed AnyBand framework. AnyBand encodes a noisy full-band spectral state together with a\n273\tclean observed low-band prompt using a frequency encoder, models temporal dependencies with a DiT backbone, and predicts\n274\tthe mel-frequency velocity field using a frequency decoder. The model is trained with an Easy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum\n275\tand further refined using multi-view adversarial objectives.\n276\tf c). The frequency mask is\n277\tc, AnyBand models pθ (M | M,\n278\tused to construct the prompt and identify the reconstruction\n279\tregion rather than being injected as a separate condition.\n280\tTraining over continuously varying cutoffs enables a single\n281\tmodel to handle diverse bandwidth settings. At inference, the\n282\tbandwidth is specified by the extent of the observed spectrum\n283\twithout requiring a separate scalar cutoff embedding.\n284\t\n285\tAnyBand\n286\tAs illustrated in Figure 2, AnyBand adopts the temporal DiT\n287\tbackbone of F5-TTS (Chen et al. 2025). Given an intermedif\n288\tate noisy spectral state Mt , the clean low-band prompt M,\n289\ta timestep t, and auxiliary conditioning information c, the\n290\tgenerator directly predicts the velocity field as:\n291\t\u0010\n292\t\u0011\n293\tf t, c .\n294\tv̂θ = Gθ Mt , M,\n295\tTo explicitly capture dependencies along the frequency axis,\n296\twe augment the backbone with a frequency encoder and decoder placed before and after the temporal DiT, respectively.\n297\tDiT Backbone. AnyBand retains the temporal DiT backbone of F5-TTS (Chen et al. 2025), including timestep conditioning and temporal self-attention, while replacing its\n298\tframe-wise mel projections with the frequency encoder and\n299\tdecoder described below. Given the noisy spectral state Mt\n300\tf the frequency encoder\n301\tand clean masked observation M,\n302\tproduces Eh ∈ RB×T ×d , which is processed by the DiT to\n303\tmodel long-range temporal dependencies.\n304\tFollowing CodecFlow (Zhang et al. 2026), we further use\n305\tF0 and voiced/unvoiced information as auxiliary conditions\n306\tfor high-frequency reconstruction. We define c = [f0 ; v],\n307\twhere f0 ∈ RB×T is the normalized F0 trajectory and\n308\tv ∈ {0, 1}B×T is the voicing indicator. Their projected embeddings are added to Eh before temporal modeling. The\n309\tresulting representations Et ∈ RB×T ×d are then mapped\n310\tback to the mel-frequency domain by the frequency decoder.\n311\tFrequency Encoder and Decoder. Bandwidth extension\n312\trequires inferring missing high-frequency content from\n313\t\n314\tfrequency-dependent cues in the observed spectrum, including harmonic structure, spectral envelopes, and cross-band\n315\tcontinuity. AnyBand therefore models the frequency axis explicitly before and after the temporal DiT backbone.\n316\tFor each frame, the frequency encoder treats the F mel\n317\tbins as frequency tokens. Values from the noisy intermediate\n318\tf are concatenated, prostate Mt and clean spectral prompt M\n319\tjected to df -dimensional embeddings, and combined with\n320\t(0)\n321\tlearned frequency positional embeddings, yielding EM ∈\n322\tRB×T ×F ×df . A shallow stack of pre-normalization Transformer blocks then updates the tokens as:\n323\t\u0010\n324\t\u0010\n325\t\u0011\u0011\n326\t(l)′\n327\t(l−1)\n328\t(l−1)\n329\tEM = EM + F-MHSA LN EM\n330\t,\n331\t\u0010\n332\t\u0010\n333\t\u0011\u0011\n334\t(2)\n335\t(l)\n336\t(l)′\n337\t(l)′\n338\tEM = EM + FFN LN EM\n339\t,\n340\twhere F-MHSA applies self-attention along the frequency\n341\taxis independently at each frame. The final frequency tokens\n342\tare aggregated through attention pooling and projected to the\n343\tDiT hidden dimension, producing Eh ∈ RB×T ×d .\n344\tAfter temporal modeling, the frequency decoder maps\n345\tEt ∈ RB×T ×d back to the mel-frequency domain. Each\n346\tframe representation is projected to the frequency hidden\n347\tdimension, broadcast over F frequency positions, and combined with learned frequency embeddings. The resulting tokens are processed by a frequency-axis Transformer stack and\n348\tmapped through a shared linear readout to produce v̂θ . Thus,\n349\tthe encoder aggregates cross-frequency evidence into framelevel representations, while the decoder predicts frequencyspecific velocities.\n350\tMulti-View Spectral Discriminators. Although the\n351\tmasked flow-matching objective provides stable reconstruction supervision, its pointwise loss may underemphasize perceptually important high-frequency details. Inspired by adversarial training in neural speech generation (Kong, Kim,\n352\tand Bae 2020; Lee et al. 2023; Jang et al. 2021; Lee, Choi,\n353\tand Lee 2024), we introduce three complementary discriminators: Dspec , Dcross , and Dharm , targeting spectral realism,\n354\t\n355\t\fDiscriminator\n356\t\n357\tAvgPool\n358\t\n359\tAvgPool\n360\t(a) Multi-Scale Spectral Discriminator\n361\t\n362\tDiscriminator\n363\t\n364\tFraction Map\n365\t\n366\tLow-band Reference\n367\t\n368\tAvgPool\n369\t\n370\tDiscriminator\n371\t\n372\t.\n373\t\n374\tSubband Grouping + Mean\n375\t\n376\tSubband Envelopes\n377\t\n378\t(b) Cross-Band Comodulation Discriminator\n379\t\n380\tConv2D\n381\tLeaky ReLU\n382\t\n383\t+\n384\t\n385\tCorrelation map\n386\t\n387\tConv2D\n388\t\n389\tV/UV Map\n390\tBroadcast\n391\t\n392\tDiscriminator\n393\t\n394\tHarmonic Amplitude Grid\n395\t\n396\tHarmonic Map\n397\t\n398\tstructed high-frequency energy is consistent with the harmonic structure implied by F0 and voicing. At frame τ ,\n399\tthe n-th harmonic frequency is fn (τ ) = nf0 (τ ). Bilinear\n400\tsampling along these trajectories yields an F0 -aligned harmonic feature grid H ∈ RNh ×T , which is stacked with an\n401\tobserved-harmonic mask and a voiced/unvoiced map before\n402\tbeing passed to Dharm . All three discriminators are implemented with lightweight 2D CNNs.\n403\t\n404\t(c) Harmonic Correspondence Discriminator\n405\t\n406\tFigure 3: Overview of the proposed discriminators, which\n407\tjointly assess multi-scale spectral realism, cross-band envelope dependencies, and F0 -aligned harmonic correspondence.\n408\t\n409\tTraining and Sampling\n410\tEasy-to-Balanced Cutoff Training. Uniform sampling\n411\tfrom the beginning may expose the model to severely bandlimited inputs before it has learned basic spectral continuation. We therefore introduce an Easy-to-Balanced curriculum that initially favors higher cutoffs and gradually transitions to uniform sampling. Given the normalized cutoff\n412\tfe = (fc − fmin )/(fmax − fmin ) ∈ [0, 1], we sample:\n413\tβeβ f\n414\tps (fe) = (1 − λs ) β\n415\t+ λs ,\n416\te −1\n417\t\u0010\n418\t\u0010\n419\t\u0011\u0011\n420\ts\n421\t1 − cos π min ρS\n422\t,1\n423\tλs =\n424\t,\n425\t2\n426\te\n427\t\n428\tcross-band envelope coherence, and harmonic correspondence, respectively.\n429\tThe multi-scale spectral discriminator Dspec follows common multi-scale designs (Kong, Kim, and Bae 2020; Jang\n430\tet al. 2021). Given a mel spectrogram M, we construct three\n431\tinputs at temporal scales of 1, 1/2, and 1/4 by average pooling along time while preserving frequency resolution. Independent branches then evaluate local time–frequency patterns under different temporal receptive fields.\n432\tMotivated by cross-frequency envelope coherence in natural sounds (Nelken, Rotman, and Yosef 1999; McDermott\n433\tand Simoncelli 2011), the cross-band discriminator Dcross\n434\tevaluates whether reconstructed high-frequency bands follow the temporal dynamics of the observed low-frequency\n435\tspectrum. We partition the F mel bins into K contiguous\n436\tsubbands {Bj }K\n437\tj=1 and compute the envelope of the j-th\n438\tsubband as:\n439\t1 X\n440\tE(j)\n441\tMk,: .\n442\t(3)\n443\ts =\n444\t|Bj |\n445\tk∈Bj\n446\t\n447\tAfter temporal standardization, the subband envelopes form\n448\tEs ∈ RK×T . We further compute the observed fraction of\n449\teach subband and the low-band reference envelope as:\n450\t1 X\n451\t(1 − Mk ) ,\n452\tρj =\n453\t|Bj |\n454\tk∈Bj\n455\t(4)\n456\tK\n457\tX\n458\tρj\n459\t(j)\n460\tEl =\n461\tEs ,\n462\tPK\n463\ti=1 ρi + ϵ\n464\tj=1\n465\t(j)\n466\t\n467\twhere Es denotes the standardized envelope. Their\n468\telement-wise products are locally averaged over time to form\n469\ta cross-band coherence map, which is combined with the\n470\tobserved-fraction map and fed to Dcross .\n471\tInspired by F0-guided source modeling (Lu, Ai, and\n472\tLing 2022; Li et al. 2023; Xu, Huang, and Zeng 2025),\n473\tthe harmonic discriminator Dharm evaluates whether recon-\n474\t\n475\t(5)\n476\t\n477\twhere β controls the initial high-cutoff bias, S is the total\n478\tnumber of training steps, and ρ controls the annealing duration. The sampled cutoff is mapped to the corresponding\n479\tmel-bin boundary to construct the frequency mask. As training progresses, the distribution smoothly approaches uniform\n480\tsampling over the full cutoff range.\n481\tMissing-Band Flow Training. Following conditional flow\n482\tmatching (Lipman et al. 2023), we sample t ∈ [0, 1) and\n483\tGaussian noise ϵ ∼ N (0, I), and construct the intermediate\n484\tnoisy state:\n485\tMt = tM + (1 − t)ϵ.\n486\t\n487\t(6)\n488\t\n489\tf remains uncorrupted and is\n490\tThe clean masked observation M\n491\tprovided separately as the spectral prompt. Under this linear\n492\tprobability path, the target velocity is:\n493\tv=\n494\t\n495\tM − Mt\n496\t= M − ϵ.\n497\t1−t\n498\t\n499\t(7)\n500\t\n501\tf t, c).\n502\tThe generator directly predicts v̂θ = Gθ (Mt , M,\n503\tSince the observed low-frequency region is already provided\n504\tf the training objective is applied only to the missthrough M,\n505\ting frequency region:\n506\t#\n507\t\"\n508\t2\n509\t∥M ⊙ (v − v̂θ )∥2\n510\tLflow = E\n511\t,\n512\t(8)\n513\tPF\n514\tT · k=1 Mk\n515\twhere the expectation is taken over training samples, cutoff\n516\tfrequencies, flow times, and Gaussian noise. The normalization by the number of missing time–frequency elements\n517\tkeeps the loss scale comparable across different cutoff frequencies.\n518\t\n519\t\fAdversarial Refinement. After flow pretraining, we refine\n520\tthe generator with the proposed multi-view spectral discriminators. Under the velocity-prediction parameterization, the\n521\tgenerator directly predicts v̂θ . At an intermediate timestep t,\n522\tthe corresponding clean-spectrogram estimate is recovered\n523\tas:\n524\tM̂ = Mt + (1 − t)v̂θ .\n525\t(9)\n526\tRather than applying adversarial supervision to arbitrary\n527\tintermediate states, we construct fake samples from the\n528\tmodel’s own sampling trajectory. We first integrate from\n529\tGaussian noise to an endpoint-near timestep te without gradient tracking, and then back-propagate through a short differentiable rollout toward the data endpoint. The generated\n530\tspectrogram is combined with the observed band as:\n531\tf + M ⊙ M̂,\n532\tM̂fb = (1 − M) ⊙ M\n533\t\n534\t(10)\n535\t\n536\tso that the known region is copied from the input and only\n537\tthe missing band is supplied by the model.\n538\tWe apply adversarial refinement near the endpoint because\n539\tearlier flow states are noisy intermediate variables rather\n540\tthan natural spectrograms (Lipman et al. 2023). This allows\n541\tthe discriminators to focus on spectral realism and highfrequency detail without back-propagating through the full\n542\tODE trajectory, following recent adversarial flow-matching\n543\taudio generation (Lee, Choi, and Lee 2024).\n544\tLet Q = {spec, cross, harm} denote the discriminator\n545\tset. For each Dq , we use a hinge GAN loss with genera(q)\n546\ttor objective Ladv = −E[Dq (M̂fb )], together with feature\n547\t(q)\n548\tmatching Lfm . The generator is optimized with:\n549\tX (q)\n550\tX (q)\n551\tLG = Lflow + λadv\n552\tLadv + λfm\n553\tLfm .\n554\t(11)\n555\tq∈Q\n556\t\n557\tq∈Q\n558\t\n559\tSampling. At inference time, AnyBand initializes the fullband state with Gaussian noise and solves the learned conditional flow over an inference time grid 0 = t0 < · · · <\n560\ttN = 1, while the clean observed low-frequency spectrum\n561\tis provided separately as the spectral prompt. At the i-th integration step, the generator directly predicts the conditional\n562\tand unconditional velocity fields, v̂icond and v̂i∅ , respectively.\n563\tWe apply classifier-free guidance in the velocity space as:\n564\t\u0010\n565\t\u0011\n566\tv̂icfg = v̂i∅ + wcfg v̂icond − v̂i∅ ,\n567\t(12)\n568\twhere wcfg denotes the guidance scale. The guided velocity\n569\tfield is integrated using the Heun solver. After the final integration step, the observed low-frequency region is restored\n570\tfrom the input prompt, while the generated result is retained\n571\tin the missing high-frequency region.\n572\t\n573\tExperiment Settings\n574\tDatasets\n575\tWe conduct experiments on the 48-kHz VCTK (Yamagishi,\n576\tVeaux, and MacDonald 2019) and EARS (Richter et al. 2024)\n577\tdatasets. VCTK speakers p225–p228 are held out for indomain evaluation, while the remaining speakers are used\n578\tfor training. For out-of-domain evaluation, we use 50 utterances from 10 EARS speakers. Band-limited inputs are\n579\t\n580\tconstructed by masking mel bins above a given cutoff. Cutoffs are sampled continuously during training and evaluated\n581\tunder both standard and irregular settings. The reported input\n582\tsampling rate equals twice the cutoff frequency.\n583\t\n584\tImplementation Details\n585\tWe use 128-bin mel spectrograms computed at 48 kHz with a\n586\t2,048-point FFT and a hop length of 256. AnyBand contains\n587\t8 temporal DiT blocks with hidden dimension 384 and 6\n588\tattention heads. Its frequency encoder and decoder each use\n589\t2 frequency-axis attention blocks with hidden dimension 64\n590\tand 4 heads. Following PACE (Zhao, Wang, and Wang 2025),\n591\tframe-level F0 and voiced/unvoiced features extracted from\n592\tthe input speech are used as auxiliary conditions. All discriminators are lightweight 2D CNNs with Leaky-ReLU activations; branches with the same architecture use independent\n593\tparameters. Training uses AdamW on eight NVIDIA L40S\n594\tGPUs with a batch size of 32 per GPU. We train the flow\n595\tmodel for 300 epochs with the Easy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum, using ρ = 0.7 and β = 4, followed by 200 epochs\n596\tof adversarial refinement with uniformly sampled continuous cutoffs. At inference, we use the Heun solver with 50\n597\tsteps and a classifier-free guidance scale of 1.4. Generated\n598\tmel spectrograms are converted to 48-kHz waveforms using\n599\tVocos2 (Siuzdak 2024).\n600\t\n601\tEvaluation Metrics\n602\tWe evaluate spectral reconstruction using log-spectral distance (LSD) (Gray and Markel 1976) and its low- and highfrequency variants (Han and Lee 2022). LSD is computed\n603\tover the full spectrum, while LF-LSD and HF-LSD measure distortion in the observed and missing frequency regions, respectively. Lower values indicate better reconstruction. We further report NISQA and its coloration dimension\n604\t(COL) (Mittag et al. 2021) for overall perceptual quality and\n605\tfrequency-response distortion, and STOI (Taal et al. 2011)\n606\tfor intelligibility preservation. For subjective evaluation, 15\n607\tlisteners rated the sound quality of outputs from each system and the ground truth on a five-point scale, using five\n608\tutterances at each input sampling rate of 8, 12, 16, 20, and\n609\t24 kHz.\n610\t\n611\tResults\n612\tMain Results\n613\tObjective Results. Table 1 compares AnyBand with representative bandwidth-extension systems (Han and Lee 2022;\n614\tLiu et al. 2024; Kim et al. 2024; Yun, Kim, and Lee 2025)\n615\ton the in-domain VCTK and out-of-domain EARS test sets.\n616\tAnyBand achieves the lowest LSD and HF-LSD across all\n617\ttested cutoff settings, demonstrating consistently strong fullband and high-frequency reconstruction. Its low LF-LSD further suggests that the observed low-frequency content is well\n618\tpreserved. The spectral gains remain consistent on EARS, indicating good cross-domain generalization, and are most pronounced under severely band-limited conditions. At higher\n619\t2\n620\t\n621\thttps://huggingface.co/kittn/vocos-mel-48khz-alpha1\n622\t\n623\t\fTable 1: Quantitative bandwidth-extension results on the in-domain VCTK and out-of-domain EARS test sets. All systems\n624\tgenerate audio at 48 kHz. The best and second-best results within each input setting are highlighted in bold and underlined,\n625\trespectively.\n626\tEARS\n627\t\n628\tVCTK\n629\t\n630\tInput SR Method\n631\t\n632\tLSD ↓ LF-LSD ↓ HF-LSD ↓ NISQA ↑ COL ↑ STOI ↑ LSD ↓ LF-LSD ↓ HF-LSD ↓ NISQA ↑ COL ↑ STOI ↑\n633\t\n634\t2 kHz\n635\t\n636\tNU-Wave 2\n637\tAudioSR\n638\tFLowHigh\n639\tFre-Painter\n640\tAnyBand (Ours)\n641\t\n642\t2.685\n643\t2.373\n644\t1.637\n645\t1.323\n646\t1.248\n647\t\n648\t1.364\n649\t0.833\n650\t0.985\n651\t1.008\n652\t0.539\n653\t\n654\t2.727\n655\t2.417\n656\t1.655\n657\t1.331\n658\t1.269\n659\t\n660\t1.897\n661\t2.356\n662\t1.840\n663\t2.697\n664\t3.125\n665\t\n666\t2.115\n667\t2.621\n668\t2.099\n669\t2.422\n670\t3.419\n671\t\n672\t0.7746\n673\t0.7913\n674\t0.7885\n675\t0.7820\n676\t0.8214\n677\t\n678\t4.039\n679\t1.958\n680\t2.304\n681\t2.568\n682\t1.546\n683\t\n684\t2.145\n685\t1.408\n686