-LSD at every tested cutoff, confirming that a single model can support nonstandard\n970\tbandwidth configurations without cutoff-specific specialization. The improvements are again larger at lower cutoffs,\n971\twhere a greater portion of the spectrum must be reconstructed, while perceptual quality and intelligibility remain\n972\tcompetitive. Overall, these results support mask-specified\n973\tspectral infilling as an effective formulation for flexible BWE\n974\tacross both standard and irregular cutoff settings.\n975\t\n976\t4.00\n977\t3.75\n978\t3.50\n979\t3.25\n980\t3.00\n981\t\n982\tGround Truth\n983\t\n984\tFre-Painter\n985\t\n986\t2.75\n987\t\n988\tNU-Wave 2\n989\tAudioSR\n990\t\n991\tFlowHigh\n992\tAnyBand (Ours)\n993\t\n994\t8\n995\t\n996\t12\n997\t\n998\t16\n999\t\n1000\t20\n1001\t\n1002\tInput Sampling Rate (kHz)\n1003\t\n1004\t24\n1005\t\n1006\tFigure 4: Subjective scores across different input sampling\n1007\trates. The dashed horizontal line denotes the ground-truth\n1008\tscore, and error bars indicate 95% confidence intervals.\n1009\t\n1010\tTo better understand the main design choices in AnyBand,\n1011\twe analyze the cutoff-sampling strategy, model components,\n1012\tprediction parameterization, and sampling configuration.\n1013\t\n1014\tuous uniform sampling covers the full cutoff range. Easyto-Balanced starts from a distribution biased toward highercutoff, less underdetermined examples and gradually transitions to continuous uniform sampling. Discrete uniform\n1015\tremains competitive at its predefined training points, while\n1016\tcontinuous uniform produces weaker average results. Easyto-Balanced achieves the best overall averages, with its largest\n1017\tgains at low and medium cutoff settings, indicating a better\n1018\tbalance across bandwidth conditions.\n1019\t\n1020\tCutoff-sampling strategy. Table 3 compares three training strategies. Discrete uniform sampling draws equivalent\n1021\tinput rates from {2, 4, 8, 16, 24, 32} kHz, whereas 