\n1416\t1.22\n1417\t\n1418\tLSD ↓ HF-LSD ↓ NISQA ↑ COL ↑ STOI ↑\n1419\t\n1420\t1.175\n1421\t1.20\n1422\t\n1423\t1.150\n1424\t1.18\n1425\t\n1426\t20\n1427\t\n1428\tNumber of Sampling Steps\n1429\t25\n1430\t\n1431\t30\n1432\t\n1433\t35\n1434\t\n1435\t40\n1436\t\n1437\t45\n1438\t\n1439\t(a) Solver and steps.\n1440\t\n1441\t1.125\n1442\t50\n1443\t\n1444\t0.4\n1445\t\n1446\t0.6\n1447\t\n1448\t0.8\n1449\t\n1450\t1.0\n1451\t\n1452\t1.2\n1453\t\n1454\t1.4\n1455\t\n1456\t1.6\n1457\t\n1458\tCFG Scale\n1459\t\n1460\t(b) Guidance scale.\n1461\t\n1462\tFigure 5: Effect of sampling configurations on HF-LSD.\n1463\tlinear input and output projections used in F5-TTS (Chen\n1464\tet al. 2025). The remaining variants remove F0/UV conditioning or one of the three discriminators. Removing the frequency modules or F0/UV conditioning generally degrades\n1465\tboth spectral and perceptual metrics. Among the discriminators, removing Dspec causes the largest overall degradation.\n1466\tAlthough removing Dcross or Dharm yields isolated improvements on individual metrics, each removal worsens the overall metric profile, suggesting that the three discriminators\n1467\tprovide complementary supervision.\n1468\tSampling configuration. Figure 5 examines the sampling\n1469\tsolver, number of steps, and classifier-free guidance scale. Increasing the number of steps consistently reduces HF-LSD\n1470\tfor both Euler and Heun, with Heun performing better across\n1471\tthe tested settings. The improvements diminish as the number of steps increases, revealing a trade-off between reconstruction quality and inference cost. Increasing the guidance\n1472\t\n1473\tscale initially improves high-frequency reconstruction, but\n1474\tthe benefit saturates and slightly degrades at larger values.\n1475\tWe therefore use the Heun solver with 50 steps and a guidance scale of 1.4.\n1476\t\n1477\tConclusion\n1478\tWe introduced AnyBand, a unified framework for speech\n1479\tbandwidth extension over continuously varying cutoff frequencies. By formulating BWE as in-context spectral infilling, AnyBand uses the observed low-frequency spectrum\n1480\tas an acoustic prompt and a cutoff-derived mask to specify the missing region, enabling a single model to handle\n1481\tdiverse bandwidth settings. Its frequency-aware encoder–\n1482\tdecoder, F0/UV conditioning, and complementary spectral,\n1483\tcross-band, and harmonic discriminators improve spectral\n1484\treconstruction and high-frequency realism. We further introduce an Easy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum and adopt velocity prediction for effective flow-based generation. Experiments on in-domain and out-of-domain datasets show that\n1485\t\n1486\t\fAnyBand consistently reduces full-band and high-frequency\n1487\tdistortion over prior systems while maintaining competitive\n1488\tperceptual quality and intelligibility. These results establish\n1489\tunified spectral infilling as an effective formulation for flexible multi-bandwidth speech extension.\n1490\t\n1491\tReferences\n1492\tAndreev, P.; Alanov, A.; Ivanov, O.; and Vetrov, D. 2023.\n1493\tHifi++: A unified framework for bandwidth extension and\n1494\tspeech enhancement. In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 1–5. IEEE.\n1495\tChen, Y.; Niu, Z.; Ma, Z.; Deng, K.; Wang, C.; JianZhao, J.;\n1496\tYu, K.; and Chen, X. 2025. F5-TTS: A Fairytaler that Fakes\n1497\tFluent and Faithful Speech with Flow Matching. In Che, W.;\n1498\tNabende, J.; Shutova, E.; and Pilehvar, M. T., eds., Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), 6255–6271.\n1499\tVienna, Austria: Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1500\tISBN 979-8-89176-251-0.\n1501\tChoi, W.; Lee, S.; Lim, H.; and Kang, H.-G. 2026. Universr:\n1502\tUnified and versatile audio super-resolution via vocoderfree flow matching. In ICASSP 2026-2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Si