sounds. Nature, 397(6715): 154–157.\n1619\t\n1620\tRichter, J.; Wu, Y.-C.; Krenn, S.; Welker, S.; Lay, B.; Watanabe, S.; Richard, A.; and Gerkmann, T. 2024. EARS: An\n1621\tAnechoic Fullband Speech Dataset Benchmarked for Speech\n1622\tEnhancement and Dereverberation. In Interspeech.\n1623\tSalhab, M.; and Harmanani, H. 2025. Speech Bandwidth\n1624\tExpansion Via High Fidelity Generative Adversarial Networks. In 2025 1st International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications (ICCIAA),\n1625\t1–7. IEEE.\n1626\tSharma, Y. 2026. Fast and Flexible Audio Bandwidth Extension via Vocos. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.07285.\n1627\tSiuzdak, H. 2024. Vocos: Closing the gap between timedomain and Fourier-based neural vocoders for high-quality\n1628\taudio synthesis. In The Twelfth International Conference on\n1629\tLearning Representations, ICLR 2024.\n1630\tSu, J.; Wang, Y.; Finkelstein, A.; and Jin, Z. 2021. Bandwidth extension is all you need. In ICASSP 2021-2021 IEEE\n1631\tInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n1632\tProcessing (ICASSP), 696–700. IEEE.\n1633\tTaal, C. H.; Hendriks, R. C.; Heusdens, R.; and Jensen, J.\n1634\t2011. An algorithm for intelligibility prediction of time–\n1635\tfrequency weighted noisy speech. IEEE Transactions on\n1636\taudio, speech, and language processing, 19(7): 2125–2136.\n1637\tWang, C.; Chen, S.; Wu, Y.; Zhang, Z.; Zhou, L.; Liu, S.;\n1638\tChen, Z.; Liu, Y.; Wang, H.; Li, J.; et al. 2023. Neural codec\n1639\tlanguage models are zero-shot text to speech synthesizers.\n1640\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2301.02111.\n1641\tXu, N.; Huang, Z.; and Zeng, X. 2025. A Universal Harmonic Discriminator for High-quality GAN-based Vocoder.\n1642\tIn IEEE Automatic Speech Recognition and Understanding\n1643\tWorkshop, ASRU 2025, 1–7. IEEE.\n1644\tYamagishi, J.; Veaux, C.; and MacDonald, K. 2019. CSTR\n1645\tVCTK Corpus: English Multi-speaker Corpus for CSTR\n1646\tVoice Cloning Toolkit (version 0.92).\n1647\tYang, N.; Li, Y.; Cuji, D. A.; Corey, R. M.; Zhao, P.; Lin, X.;\n1648\tand Singer, A. C. 2026. A survey of advancing audio superresolution and bandwidth extension from discriminative to\n1649\tgenerative models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.16681.\n1650\tYun, J.-H.; Kim, S.-B.; and Lee, S.-W. 2025. FLowHigh: Towards efficient and high-quality audio super-resolution with\n1651\tsingle-step flow matching. In ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 1–5. IEEE.\n1652\tZhang, B.; Zhao, J.; McLoughlin, I.; Wang, Y.; and Madhukumar, A. 2026. Codecflow: Efficient bandwidth extension via conditional flow matching in neural codec latent\n1653\tspace. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.02022.\n1654\tZhang, K.; Ren, Y.; Xu, C.; and Zhao, Z. 2021. WSRGlow: A Glow-Based Waveform Generative Model for Audio Super-Resolution. In 22nd Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association, Interspeech\n1655\t2021, 1649–1653. ISCA.\n1656\tZhao, J.; Vu, M. D.; and Wang, Y. 2026. Hierarchical Decoding for Discrete Speech Synthesis with Multi-Resolution\n1657\tSpoof Detection. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.05373.\n1658\t\n1659\t\fZhao, J.; Wang, X.; and Wang, Y. 2025. Prosody-Adaptable\n1660\tAudio Codecs for Zero-Shot Voice Conversion via InContext Learning. In 26th Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association, Interspeech\n1661\t2025. ISCA.\n1662\tZhao, J.; Zeng, W.; Lyu, T.; and Wang, Y. 2026. Comelsinger:\n1663\tDiscrete token-based zero-shot singing synthesis with structured melody control and guidance. IEEE Transactions on\n1664\tAudio, Speech and Language Processing.\n1665\t\n1666\tA\n1667\tA.1\n1668\t\n1669\tAdditional Implementation Details\n1670\t\n1671\tData and Acoustic Features\n1672\t\n1673\tAll speech signals are processed at 48 kHz. During training,\n1674\twe randomly extract waveform segments of 64,000 samples.\n1675\tWe follow the acoustic preprocessing configuration of the\n1676\t48-kHz Vocos model and compute 128-bin mel spectrograms\n1677\tusing a 2,048-point FFT and a hop length of 256. The mel\n1678\tfilterbank covers 0–24 kHz and uses a magnitude power of\n1679\t1.0, Slaney normalization, and the Slaney mel scale.\n1680\tThe cutoff frequency is independently sampled for each\n1681\ttraining segment from the continuous range of 1–16 kHz.\n1682\tThe corresponding mel-bin boundary is determined by comparing the center frequency of each mel bin with the sampled\n1683\tcutoff, following the mask definition in the main paper.\n1684\tFrame-level F0 and voiced/unvoiced features are extracted\n1685\tfrom the corresponding band-limited input waveform using\n1686\tpYIN, with YIN used as a fallback when pYIN does not return a valid estimate. For voiced frames, F0 is logarithmically\n1687\tnormalized as:\n1688\t\u0012\n1689\t\u0013\n1690\tlog f0 − log 86\n1691\te\n1692\tf0 = clip\n1693\t, 0, 1 .\n1694\t(13)\n1695\tlog 1100 − log 86\n1696\tThe normalized F0 value is set to zero for unvoiced frames,\n1697\twhile a separate binary feature indicates the voiced/unvoiced\n1698\tstate.\n1699\t\n1700\tA.2\n1701\t\n1702\tModel Configuration\n1703\t\n1704\tGenerator. The AnyBand generator contains approximately 37 million parameters. Its temporal backbone consists of eight DiT blocks with a hidden dimension of 384\n1705\tand six attention heads. The frequency encoder and decoder\n1706\teach contain two frequency-axis Transformer blocks with a\n1707\thidden dimension of 64 and four attention heads. The frequency encoder takes the intermediate noisy state Mt and\n1708\tf as input. Their values are\n1709\tthe observed spectral prompt M\n1710\tconcatenated at each time–frequency position and projected\n1711\tinto frequency-token embeddings. After frequency-axis selfattention, a learned query performs four-head attention pooling over the frequency tokens to obtain a frame-level representation. The temporal DiT then models long-range dependencies across frames, after which the frequency decoder\n1712\tproduces frequency-specific velocity predictions. The frequency mask is used to construct the observed prompt and\n1713\tdefine the reconstruction region, but is not directly injected\n1714\tinto the generator.\n1715\t\n1716\tDiscriminators. The multi-scale spectral discriminator\n1717\tDspec evaluates mel spectrograms at three temporal resolutions. Each resolution is processed by an independent 2D\n1718\tconvolutional branch with channel dimensions of 32, 64, 128,\n1719\tand 256. The cross-band discriminator Dcross partitions the\n1720\t128 mel bins into 16 contiguous subbands, each containing\n1721\teight mel bins, and computes local cross-band statistics using\n1722\ta temporal window of 32 frames. The harmonic discriminator Dharm considers the first 12 F0-aligned harmonics, whose\n1723\tamplitudes are bilinearly sampled from the mel spectrogram.\n1724\tThe three discriminators use separate, task-specific 2D convolutional networks.\n1725\t\n1726\tA.3\n1727\t\n1728\tTraining Configuration\n1729\t\n1730\tFlow Pretraining. We first train the generator for 300\n1731\tepochs using the missing-band velocity-prediction objective described in the main paper. The model is optimized\n1732\twith AdamW using an initial learning rate of 10−3 and a\n1733\tcosine-annealing learning-rate schedule with Tmax = 300.\n1734\tNo learning-rate warmup is used. The Easy-to-Balanced cutoff curriculum uses β = 4 and ρ = 0.7.\n1735\tAdversarial Refinement. The pretrained generator is subsequently refined for 200 epochs using the three proposed\n1736\tdiscriminators. During this stage, cutoff frequencies are uniformly sampled from the continuous training range. The\n1737\tgenerator and discriminators are optimized with AdamW\n1738\tusing learning rates of 5 × 10−5 and 2 × 10−4 , respectively,\n1739\twith (β1 , β2 ) = (0.8, 0.99). Both optimizers use cosineannealing schedules with Tmax = 200 and no learning-rate\n1740\twarmup. The generator and discriminators are updated once\n1741\tper batch in a 1:1 ratio. We set the adversarial-loss weight to\n1742\tλadv = 0.1 and equally weight the adversarial losses from\n1743\tthe three discriminators. The feature-matching weight is set\n1744\tto λfm = 2.0, with feature-matching losses summed over\n1745\ttheir intermediate feature maps.\n1746\tFor endpoint-focused adversarial refinement, we first integrate the flow from Gaussian noise to te = 0.8 without\n1747\tgradient tracking. Starting from the resulting intermediate\n1748\tstate, we perform an eight-step differentiable Heun rollout\n1749\tover [te , 1]. The adversarial and feature-matching objectives\n1750\tare applied to the resulting full-band estimate, with the observed frequency region copied from the band-limited input\n1751\tas defined in the main paper.\n1752\tComputational Setup. AnyBand is trained with bfloat16\n1753\tmixed precision on eight NVIDIA L40S GPUs, using a batch\n1754\tsize of 32 per GPU and a global batch size of 256.\n1755\t\n1756\tA.4\n1757\t\n1758\tInference Configuration\n1759\t\n1760\tAt inference time, we use the Heun solver with 50 sampling\n1761\tsteps and a right-swayed time grid with γ = 2.0. Classifierfree guidance is applied in velocity space with a guidance\n1762\tscale of 1.4. The observed spectral prompt is provided separately throughout sampling. The observed frequency region\n1763\tis not overwritten after each intermediate integration step\n1764\tand is copied back only after the final step. We generate one\n1765\toutput for each utterance in both objective and subjective\n1766\tevaluations.\n1767\t\n1768\t\fB\n1769\tB.1\n1770\t\n1771\tExperimental Details\n1772\t\n1773\tBaseline Configurations\n1774\t\n1775\tCommon Protocol. All baseline systems are implemented\n1776\tusing their official codebases. We use the same VCTK training split, training utterances, held-out test utterances, and\n1777\tnominal cutoff settings for all methods. To preserve the original formulation of each baseline, band-limited inputs are\n1778\tconstructed using the native preprocessing procedure provided by its official implementation. Methods initialized from\n1779\tpublicly released checkpoints are subsequently fine-tuned on\n1780\tthe shared VCTK training split, whereas the remaining methods are trained from scratch on the same data. All systems\n1781\tgenerate 48-kHz outputs and are evaluated using the same\n1782\tmetric protocols.\n1783\tNU-Wave 2 (Han and Lee 2022). We use the official\n1784\timplementation of NU-Wave 23 and train the model from\n1785\tscratch on the shared VCTK training split. We retain its original bandwidth-conditioning mechanism and native waveform preprocessing procedure, while adapting the training\n1786\tand evaluation cutoff settings to our experimental protocol.\n1787\tAudioSR (Liu et al. 2024). We use the official AudioSR\n1788\timplementation and its publicly released checkpoint4 , followed by fine-tuning on the shared VCTK training split. We\n1789\tretain the official input preprocessing and recommended inference configuration.\n1790\tFLowHigh (Yun, Kim, and Lee 2025). We use the official FLowHigh implementation5 and initialize the model\n1791\tfrom the released checkpoint6 . The model is then fine-tuned\n1792\ton the shared VCTK training split while retaining its original\n1793\tprobability path, input preprocessing, and inference procedure.\n1794\tFre-Painter (Kim et al. 2024). We use the official implementation of Fre-Painter7 and train the model from scratch\n1795\ton the shared VCTK training split. We retain its original\n1796\tpreprocessing, training objective, and inference procedure,\n1797\twhile adapting the training and evaluation bandwidth settings to our experimental protocol.\n1798\t\n1799\tB.2\n1800\t\n1801\tAblation Configurations\n1802\t\n1803\tComponent Ablations. For the variant without frequency\n1804\tmodules, we replace the proposed frequency encoder and\n1805\tdecoder with the frame-wise linear input and output projections used in the original temporal DiT backbone. The variant\n1806\twithout F0/VUV removes both auxiliary conditioning features while retaining the remaining generator architecture.\n1807\tTo evaluate the contribution of each adversarial objective,\n1808\twe remove one discriminator at a time during adversarial\n1809\trefinement while retaining the other two discriminators. All\n1810\tdiscriminator ablations are initialized from the same Stage1 flow checkpoint and independently refined for the same\n1811\t\n1812\ttraining steps. The remaining training data, cutoff-sampling\n1813\tstrategy, optimization settings, and inference configuration\n1814\tare kept identical to those of the full model.\n1815\tCutoff-Sampling Strategies. We compare three cutoffsampling strategies during Stage-1 flow pretraining. Discrete Uniform uniformly samples equivalent input rates from\n1816\t{2, 4, 8, 16, 24, 32} kHz, corresponding to cutoff frequencies of {1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16} kHz. Continuous Uniform samples\n1817\tcutoff frequencies uniformly from the full continuous range\n1818\tof 1–16 kHz throughout training. Easy-to-Balanced initially\n1819\tfavors higher cutoff frequencies and gradually transitions to\n1820\tthe same continuous uniform distribution.\n1821\tContinuous Uniform and Easy-to-Balanced share the same\n1822\tcontinuous cutoff support and differ only in how the sampling distribution evolves during Stage-1 training. All three\n1823\tvariants use the same model architecture, training data, optimization settings, and training budget. They are then refined\n1824\tusing the same Stage-2 adversarial-training configuration, in\n1825\twhich cutoff frequencies are uniformly sampled from the\n1826\tcontinuous training range. Therefore, the compared models\n1827\tdiffer only in the cutoff-sampling strategy used during Stage1 flow pretraining.\n1828\t\n1829\tB.3\n1830\t\n1831\tObjective Evaluation Protocol\n1832\t\n1833\tSpectral Metrics. LSD, LF-LSD, and HF-LSD are combt,k\n1834\tputed from the final 48-kHz waveforms. Let Xt,k and X\n1835\tdenote the complex STFT coefficients of the reference and\n1836\tgenerated waveforms at time frame t and frequency bin k,\n1837\trespectively. We first compute their log-power spectra as:\n1838\t\u0001\u0001\n1839\tYt,k = log10 max |Xt,k |2 , ϵ ,\n1840\t(14)\n1841\twhere ϵ = 10−8 . The STFT uses a 2,048-sample Hann window with a hop size of 512 samples. For a frequency-bin set\n1842\tK, the corresponding spectral distance is defined as:\n1843\tT s\n1844\t\u00112\n1845\t1X\n1846\t1 X \u0010b\n1847\td(K) =\n1848\tYt,k − Yt,k .\n1849\t(15)\n1850\tT t=1 |K|\n1851\tk∈K\n1852\t\n1853\tGiven the input cutoff frequency fc , LSD is computed over\n1854\tthe full frequency range from 0 to 24 kHz. LF-LSD is computed over frequency bins at or below fc , whereas HF-LSD\n1855\tis computed over frequency bins above fc and up to 24 kHz.\n1856\tReference and generated signals are converted to mono before evaluation. When their lengths differ, the longer signal is\n1857\ttrimmed to the length of the shorter one, without additional\n1858\ttemporal alignment.\n1859\tPerceptual Quality and Intelligibility Metrics. We report the overall quality score and coloration dimension predicted by NISQA8 as NISQA and COL, respectively. Speech\n1860\tintelligibility is evaluated using the standard STOI metric.\n1861\tAll metrics are computed utterance-wise and then averaged\n1862\tover the complete evaluation set.\n1863\t\n1864\t3\n1865\t\n1866\thttps://github.com/maum-ai/nuwave2\n1867\thttps://github.com/haoheliu/versatile_audio_super_resolution\n1868\t5\n1869\thttps://github.com/resemble-ai/flowhigh\n1870\t6\n1871\thttps://drive.google.com/drive/folders/\n1872\t1clsJ3bFTZSCLOb4I0wk0l6FL9Yh33Vof\n1873\t7\n1874\thttps://github.com/ishine/FrePainter\n1875\t4\n1876\t\n1877\tB.4\n1878\t\n1879\tSubjective Evaluation Protocol\n1880\t\n1881\tWe conduct a listening test to evaluate the overall speech\n1882\tquality of the bandwidth-extended speech. The evaluation\n1883\t8\n1884\t\n1885\thttps://github.com/gabrielmittag/NISQA\n1886\t\n1887\t\f4\n1888\t\n1889\tSampling density ps (f)̃\n1890\t\n1891\tEarly (s = 0, high-cutoff-biased)\n1892\tMiddle (s = 0.3ρS)\n1893\tLate (s = 0.7ρS)\n1894\t\n1895\t3\n1896\t\n1897\tAfter transition (s ≥ ρS, uniform)\n1898\t\n1899\t2\n1900\t\n1901\t1\n1902\t\n1903\t0\n1904\t0.0\n1905\t\n1906\tFigure 6: Instructions for the subjective listening test.\n1907\t\n1908\t0.2\n1909\t\n1910\tC.1\n1911\t\n1912\tcovers five input sampling rates: 8, 12, 16, 20, and 24 kHz. For\n1913\teach input rate, five utterances are selected, and the outputs\n1914\tof five systems are evaluated. The original 48-kHz recordings\n1915\tare also included as reference samples. A total of 15 listeners\n1916\tparticipate in the evaluation, with each listener rating 130\n1917\taudio samples.\n1918\tThe test takes approximately 40 minutes and is divided\n1919\tinto three sections, with optional breaks between sections.\n1920\tParticipants are instructed to conduct the test in a quiet environment, use headphones or earphones, maintain a comfortable and consistent playback volume, and listen to each\n1921\tcomplete sample before assigning a score. Each sample is\n1922\tevaluated independently using a five-point overall-speechquality scale: 5 (Excellent), 4 (Good), 3 (Fair), 2 (Poor),\n1923\tand 1 (Bad). Participants are asked to focus only on speech\n1924\tquality and disregard the linguistic content, accent, speaking\n1925\tstyle, and speaker identity. The final score of each system\n1926\tis obtained by averaging the ratings across utterances and\n1927\tlisteners. The listening-test instructions and sample-rating\n1928\tinterface are shown in Figure 6 and 7, respectively.\n1929\t\n1930\t0.6\n1931\t\n1932\t0.8\n1933\t\n1934\t1.0\n1935\t\n1936\tFigure 8: Illustration of the Easy-to-Balanced cutoffsampling curriculum. Early training emphasizes larger normalized cutoffs, corresponding to easier inputs with narrower\n1937\tmissing bands. The sampling density gradually transitions\n1938\tto a continuous uniform distribution, which is used after\n1939\ts ≥ ρS.\n1940\t\n1941\tC\n1942\t\n1943\tFigure 7: Interface for rating overall speech quality.\n1944\t\n1945\t0.4\n1946\t\n1947\tNormalized cutoff f ̃\n1948\t\n1949\tAdditional Experimental Analyses\n1950\t\n1951\tCutoff-Sampling Curriculum Analysis\n1952\t\n1953\tCutoff-Sampling Curriculum. Figure 8 illustrates the\n1954\tproposed Easy-to-Balanced cutoff-sampling schedule. At the\n1955\tbeginning of training, the sampling density is biased toward larger normalized cutoffs, which correspond to easier\n1956\tbandwidth-extension conditions with a smaller missing frequency region. As training proceeds, the distribution gradually interpolates toward the continuous uniform distribution\n1957\tover the full cutoff range. After the transition stage ends at\n1958\ts = ρS, the model is trained using uniform continuous cutoff sampling. This schedule provides a smooth progression\n1959\tfrom easier to more underdetermined bandwidth conditions,\n1960\twhile ultimately preserving balanced coverage of the entire\n1961\ttraining range.\n1962\tIrregular-Bandwidth Evaluation. Table 5 extends the\n1963\tcutoff-sampling comparison to irregular input sampling\n1964\trates. Unlike the regular-bandwidth results, where Continuous Uniform does not consistently outperform Discrete Uniform, Continuous Uniform achieves lower LSD and HF-LSD\n1965\tacross all irregular settings. This contrast highlights the importance of exposing the model to continuously varying cutoffs when generalizing beyond the discrete training points.\n1966\tEasy-to-Balanced further obtains the lowest LSD and HFLSD at every evaluated bandwidth, showing that its advantage cannot be attributed solely to continuous cutoff coverage.\n1967\tThe gains are particularly evident at the more challenging 3and 6-kHz inputs, where it also achieves the best perceptual and intelligibility scores. Although Continuous Uniform\n1968\tis slightly better on several perceptual metrics at higher input bandwidths, Easy-to-Balanced achieves the best average\n1969\tresult across all reported metrics.\n1970\tBandwidth-wise Effects of Easy-to-Balanced Training.\n1971\tTo examine how different bandwidth conditions evolve un-\n1972\t\n1973\t\fTable 5: Ablation study of cutoff-sampling strategies on\n1974\tVCTK. Input sampling rates of 3, 6, 12, and 15 kHz correspond to Nyquist cutoffs of 1.5, 3, 6, and 7.5 kHz, respectively. The output sampling rate is 48 kHz. The best and\n1975\tsecond-best results within each input setting are highlighted\n1976\tin bold and underlined, respectively. Avg. denotes the macroaverage over the four input sampling rates.\n1977\tInput SR Sampling strategy\n1978\t\n1979\tLSD ↓ HF-LSD ↓ NISQA ↑ COL ↑ STOI ↑\n1980\t\n1981\t3 kHz\n1982\t\n1983\tUniform (discrete)\n1984\t1.280\n1985\tUniform (continuous) 1.265\n1986\tEasy-to-Balanced\n1987\t1.228\n1988\t\n1989\t1.321\n1990\t1.305\n1991\t1.261\n1992\t\n1993\t3.511\n1994\t3.574\n1995\t3.671\n1996\t\n1997\t3.602\n1998\t3.464\n1999\t3.702\n2000\t\n2001\t0.8454\n2002\t0.8485\n2003\t0.8523\n2004\t\n2005\t6 kHz\n2006\t\n2007\tUniform (discrete)\n2008\t1.185\n2009\tUniform (continuous) 1.156\n2010\tEasy-to-Balanced\n2011\t1.123\n2012\t\n2013\t1.249\n2014\t1.218\n2015\t1.177\n2016\t\n2017\t3.709\n2018\t3.888\n2019\t4.060\n2020\t\n2021\t3.654\n2022\t3.674\n2023\t4.002\n2024\t\n2025\t0.9412\n2026\t0.9505\n2027\t0.9575\n2028\t\n2029\t12 kHz\n2030\t\n2031\tUniform (discrete)\n2032\t1.152\n2033\tUniform (continuous) 1.021\n2034\tEasy-to-Balanced\n2035\t1.002\n2036\t\n2037\t1.210\n2038\t1.102\n2039\t1.093\n2040\t\n2041\t3.894\n2042\t3.922\n2043\t3.987\n2044\t\n2045\t3.825\n2046\t3.954\n2047\t3.920\n2048\t\n2049\t0.9904\n2050\t0.9985\n2051\t0.9968\n2052\t\n2053\t15 kHz\n2054\t\n2055\tUniform (discrete)\n2056\t1.019\n2057\tUniform (continuous) 0.993\n2058\tEasy-to-Balanced\n2059\t0.975\n2060\t\n2061\t1.123\n2062\t1.092\n2063\t1.085\n2064\t\n2065\t3.861\n2066\t4.034\n2067\t3.975\n2068\t\n2069\t3.956\n2070\t4.009\n2071\t3.894\n2072\t\n2073\t0.9905\n2074\t0.9975\n2075\t0.9987\n2076\t\n2077\tAvg.\n2078\t\n2079\tUniform (discrete)\n2080\t1.159\n2081\tUniform (continuous) 1.109\n2082\tEasy-to-Balanced\n2083\t1.082\n2084\t\n2085\t1.226\n2086\t1.179\n2087\t1.154\n2088\t\n2089\t3.744\n2090\t3.855\n2091\t3.923\n2092\t\n2093\t3.759\n2094\t3.775\n2095\t3.880\n2096\t\n2097\t0.9419\n2098\t0.9488\n2099\t0.9513\n2100\t\n2101\tder Easy-to-Balanced training, we track the full-band LSD\n2102\tthroughout Stage-1 training at input sampling rates of 2, 4,\n2103\t8, 16, and 24 kHz. Figure 9 reports both the absolute LSD\n2104\tand its relative reduction with respect to the first evaluation\n2105\tcheckpoint. For an input sampling rate s, the relative LSD\n2106\treduction at epoch e is defined as\n2107\tRe(s) =\n2108\t\n2109\t(s)\n2110\tLSD(s)\n2111\te0 − LSDe\n2112\t\n2113\tLSD(s)\n2114\te0\n2115\t\n2116\t× 100%,\n2117\t\n2118\t(16)\n2119\t\n2120\twhere e0 denotes the first evaluation checkpoint.\n2121\tAs shown in Figure 9a, lower input sampling rates consistently result in higher absolute LSD, reflecting the greater\n2122\tdifficulty of reconstructing a wider missing frequency region. After accounting for these different initial error levels,\n2123\tFigure 9b shows that the challenging 2-, 4-, and 8-kHz conditions achieve larger overall relative reductions than the 16and 24-kHz conditions. The high-bandwidth conditions remain comparatively stable, while the low-bandwidth curves\n2124\texhibit larger improvements together with some local fluctuations. This behavior is consistent with the intended curriculum: training begins with relatively informative inputs and\n2125\tprogressively increases exposure to more severely underdetermined bandwidth conditions before reaching a balanced\n2126\tcontinuous cutoff distribution.\n2127\t\n2128\tC.2\n2129\t\n2130\tComponent Ablation across Input\n2131\tBandwidths\n2132\t\n2133\tTable 6 extends the component ablation to multiple input sampling rates (2, 4, and 8 kHz). Removing either the\n2134\tfrequency-aware modules or the F0/VUV conditioning generally degrades spectral reconstruction, perceptual quality,\n2135\t\n2136\tand intelligibility, with the effects being most pronounced\n2137\tunder severely bandwidth-limited inputs. This indicates that\n2138\texplicit frequency-wise modeling and pitch–voicing cues become particularly important when only a small portion of the\n2139\toriginal spectrum is observed. Their influence becomes less\n2140\tpronounced as the available bandwidth increases, although\n2141\tthe complete model provides a more balanced performance\n2142\tacross the reported metrics.\n2143\tAmong the adversarial components, removing Dspec results in the most consistent degradation across input bandwidths, confirming the importance of multi-scale spectral\n2144\trealism during adversarial refinement. The contributions of\n2145\tDcross and Dharm are more dependent on the bandwidth and\n2146\tevaluation metric. Removing either component occasionally\n2147\timproves an individual metric at higher input sampling rates,\n2148\tbut generally weakens perceptual quality and performance\n2149\tunder more challenging low-bandwidth conditions. Overall,\n2150\tthe complete AnyBand model achieves the best average performance across all reported metrics, suggesting that the proposed components provide complementary benefits across\n2151\tdifferent degrees of bandwidth limitation.\n2152\t\n2153\tC.3\n2154\t\n2155\tPrediction Parameterization\n2156\t\n2157\tCompared Parameterizations. We further compare vprediction, which directly estimates the flow velocity (Lipman et al. 2023), with x-prediction, which predicts the clean\n2158\tspectrogram and converts it to velocity for flow matching\n2159\tand ODE sampling, following JiT (Li and He 2026). The\n2160\ttwo variants use the same probability path, velocity-space\n2161\ttraining objective, model architecture, and sampling procedure, differing only in the quantity directly predicted by the\n2162\tgenerator.\n2163\tUnder v-prediction, the generator directly estimates the\n2164\tvelocity field:\n2165\t\u0010\n2166\t\u0011\n2167\tf t, c .\n2168\tbθ = Gθ Mt , M,\n2169\tv\n2170\t(17)\n2171\tUnder x-prediction, the generator instead predicts the clean\n2172\tfull-band spectrogram:\n2173\t\u0010\n2174\t\u0011\n2175\tc θ = Gθ Mt , M,\n2176\tf t, c ,\n2177\tM\n2178\t(18)\n2179\twhich is converted into the corresponding velocity as:\n2180\tc θ − Mt\n2181\tM\n2182\t,\n2183\tδt = max(1 − t, δ).\n2184\t(19)\n2185\tδt\n2186\tThe resulting velocity is used for both the masked flowmatching objective and ODE sampling. Although the two\n2187\tparameterizations are algebraically equivalent under perfect\n2188\tprediction, they impose different prediction targets on the\n2189\tgenerator. v-prediction directly estimates the vector field required by the ODE solver, whereas x-prediction first estimates the clean endpoint and then applies a time-dependent\n2190\tconversion to obtain the velocity.\n2191\tbθ =\n2192\tv\n2193\t\n2194\tResults. Table 7 compares the two parameterizations under otherwise identical configurations. At 32 sampling steps,\n2195\tv-prediction outperforms x-prediction across all reported\n2196\tmetrics. At 16 steps, v-prediction continues to achieve better LSD, HF-LSD, COL, and STOI, whereas x-prediction\n2197\t\n2198\t\f2 kHz\n2199\t\n2200\t8 kHz\n2201\t\n2202\t4 kHz\n2203\t\n2204\t16 kHz\n2205\t\n2206\tRelative LSD Reduction (%)\n2207\t\n2208\t1.4\n2209\t\n2210\t24 kHz\n2211\t\n2212\tLSD\n2213\t\n2214\t1.2\n2215\t\n2216\t1.0\n2217\t\n2218\t0.8\n2219\t\n2220\t0\n2221\t\n2222\t50\n2223\t\n2224\t100\n2225\t\n2226\t150\n2227\t\n2228\t200\n2229\t\n2230\t250\n2231\t\n2232\t300\n2233\t\n2234\t25%\n2235\t20%\n2236\t\n2237\t2 kHz\n2238\t\n2239\t8 kHz\n2240\t\n2241\t4 kHz\n2242\t\n2243\t16 kHz\n2244\t\n2245\t24 kHz\n2246\t\n2247\t15%\n2248\t10%\n2249\t5%\n2250